## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-region data residency, cross-subscription CI/CD, and security controls.

## Key Concepts
- Cosmos DB with RLS and multi-region writes
- API Management as API facade
- Azure Front Door for global routing and TLS termination
- Terraform + GitHub Actions across subscriptions
- Data residency and backup strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Terraform-like snippet (HCL/Terraform)
provider "azurerm" {
  features = {}
}
resource "azurerm_cosmosdb_account" "example" {
  name                = "example-cosmos"
  location            = "East US"
  locations           = [
    { location_name = "East US" },
    { location_name = "West Europe" }
  ]
  kind                          = "GlobalDocumentDB"
  enable_multiple_write_locations = true
  consistency_policy            = { consistency_level = "Session" }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test DR failover and data latency?
- How would you enforce per-tenant data access at API level?
- How would you monitor cost and usage across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to assemble a cohesive, beginner-friendly Azure stack with core services, PaaS patterns, security, monitoring, and basic DR.

## Key Concepts
- Static Web Apps, Functions (Consumption), Cosmos DB (SQL API)
- Front Door TLS endpoint, GitHub Actions CI/CD
- Application Insights, Azure Key Vault
- Tagging, budgets, basic cross-region DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// example: read a Key Vault secret with Azure SDK for JS
const { SecretClient } = require("@azure/keyvault-secrets");
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require("@azure/identity");
const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const client = new SecretClient("https://<vault-name>.vault.azure.net", credential);
const secret = await client.getSecret("DbPassword");
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize costs if traffic spikes unexpectedly?
- What monitoring metrics would you surface in dashboards and alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question checks hands-on familiarity with Azure Monitor and Application Insights integration across a simple stack, plus the practicalities of centralizing telemetry in Log Analytics and building dashboards and alerts. It also touches cost considerations via sampling.

## Key Concepts
- Application Insights integration for SPA (JS) and Azure Functions (Node/JS)
- Log Analytics workspace as telemetry sink
- Azure Monitor dashboards and alert rules (latency, error rate)
- Telemetry sampling strategy and cost/visibility trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Frontend: initialize App Insights
import { ApplicationInsights } from '@microsoft/applicationinsights-web';
const appInsights = new ApplicationInsights({ config: { instrumentationKey: '<INSTRUMENTATION_KEY>' }});
appInsights.loadAppInsights();
appInsights.trackPageView();
```

```js
// Backend: Azure Functions (Node)
const appInsights = require('applicationinsights');
appInsights.setup(process.env.APPINSIGHTS_INSTRUMENTATIONKEY).start();
const client = appInsights.defaultClient;
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  client.trackEvent({ name: 'HttpRequest', properties: { path: req.url }});
  context.res = { status: 200, body: 'ok' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust sampling to control cost while preserving signal?
- What criteria determine which endpoints require 100% telemetry?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of observability in a beginner Azure stack, ensuring the candidate can wire instrumentation, telemetry exports, and basic dashboards end-to-end.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry integration in Azure Functions
- Application Insights exporter and per-endpoint metrics
- Sampling, traces, and dashboards in Azure Monitor
- GitHub Actions CI/CD for deployments
- Front Door TLS endpoint configuration
- SQL Auto Failover Group for cross-region DR

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry setup (Node.js)
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } = require('@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node');
const { AzureMonitorTraceExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-azure-monitor');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  traceExporter: new AzureMonitorTraceExporter({
    connectionString: process.env.APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING
  }),
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  sampler: new AlwaysParentSampler(0.2) // 20% sampling
});

sdk.start();
```
